{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The null check that "looked unnecessary"</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      You ask an AI assistant to "clean up" a price-formatting function. It comes back with
      one line removed: a guard clause that returned <code>—</code> when the price hadn't
      loaded yet. The suggestion applies straight into your editor, no diff, no confirmation.
      You skim the new version, it looks fine, you commit. Three days later prices are
      showing <code>$NaN</code> for every product still loading on slow connections.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The AI wasn't wrong about the code being simpler without the null check — it was wrong
      about the check being unnecessary. It had no way to know <code>cents</code> genuinely
      arrives as <code>null</code> during the loading state; that context lives in the
      calling component, not the function it was looking at. Nothing failed loudly. The
      function still returned a string, just the wrong one.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is the specific failure mode diff-review UIs exist to catch: not "the AI is
      usually wrong" (it isn't), but that an auto-applied edit removes the one thing a
      human would have caught in half a second — a removed guard clause is visually obvious
      in a diff, and invisible in a finished file you're skimming for the first time.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Show the change, require an explicit decision</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The fix isn't "don't use AI suggestions" — it's changing how a suggestion reaches your
      file. Instead of the AI editing the file directly, it proposes a diff: here's what
      would be removed, here's what would be added. You look at exactly those lines, and
      you click Accept or Reject. Nothing merges until you've made that call.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      This is the same review discipline you'd apply to a human teammate's pull request,
      pointed at an AI suggestion instead — and it's exactly the shape GitHub Copilot's own
      review UI uses: suggestions render as inline diffs with per-suggestion Accept/Dismiss
      controls, not as silent file edits.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The mechanism that matters isn't the diff rendering, it's the required decision point.
      A diff you can passively scroll past provides no more protection than an auto-applied
      edit — the review has to block on an explicit accept or reject per change before it
      takes effect, which is what turns "the AI proposed something" into "a human confirmed
      this specific change is correct."
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The edit that shipped because nothing looked different</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A small e-commerce team wired an in-house AI "quick fix" button directly into their
      code editor: click it, the suggestion replaces the selected function in place. It
      worked well for weeks — mostly small renames and obvious cleanups. Then someone used
      it on <code>formatPrice()</code>, and the null-check removal above happened for real.
      The PR that shipped it had a green CI run and a one-line diff in GitHub — but the
      developer had already accepted the AI's in-editor rewrite before opening the PR, so by
      the time a teammate could have caught it in review, the change looked like an
      intentional simplification, not a suggestion someone should double-check.
    </p>
    <p>
      Support tickets about "$NaN" showing up on the pricing page took four days to connect
      back to that commit, because nothing in the deploy pipeline flagged it — the code was
      syntactically fine and passed every existing test, none of which covered the
      still-loading state.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The postmortem's actual finding wasn't "the AI made a mistake" — it was that the
      tool's UX made an AI suggestion indistinguishable from a change the developer had
      written and verified themselves. There was no moment in the workflow where the
      specific lines being removed were shown side-by-side with a decision required.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The fix the team shipped wasn't a smarter model — it was a UI change: the "quick fix"
      button now opens a diff panel with the proposed removal/addition highlighted and
      Accept/Reject buttons, and nothing touches the file until one is clicked. The bug rate
      from AI-assisted edits didn't drop because suggestions got better; it dropped because
      rejecting a bad one became a five-second, zero-cost action instead of something that
      required noticing a problem after the fact.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

