{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Making the review step real, not decorative</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A diff view only helps if someone actually reads it. Highlight exactly the changed
      lines (not the whole function) so the reviewer's eyes go straight to what matters, and
      make Reject at least as easy to reach as Accept — a UI that buries "reject" one click
      deeper than "accept" quietly nudges people toward rubber-stamping.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Keep an audit trail of what was accepted vs. rejected and by whom, scoped per
      suggestion, not per session — this is what lets a postmortem answer "did anyone
      actually see this diff" instead of guessing from git blame alone.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The diff panel needs the same accessibility treatment as any other critical-path UI:
      the accept/reject controls must be reachable and operable by keyboard alone (this
      demo's buttons are real <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> elements for exactly that reason),
      and the applied/rejected result should announce via <code>aria-live</code> so a screen
      reader user gets the same "did my decision take effect" confirmation a sighted user
      gets from watching the code visibly change.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

