{# ── Chapter: Takeaway ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="takeaway">
  <h2>Remember</h2>
  <div class="case-takeaway">
    <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">An AI suggestion that edits your file directly, with no diff shown, removes the one moment you'd naturally catch a bad change.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Route AI suggestions through the same diff-and-decide flow you'd use for a human teammate's PR — GitHub Copilot's own review UI works exactly this way.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">The protection comes from a required per-hunk decision blocking the merge, not from the diff rendering itself — a diff you can passively scroll past protects nothing.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">A removed guard clause looks obvious in a diff and invisible once it's already part of a finished file you're skimming for the first time.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">The AI wasn't wrong that the code was simpler — it was wrong about the check being unused, because that context lived in a caller it never saw.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">Make Reject at least as reachable as Accept, and keep an audit trail of which suggestions were actually reviewed — both close real gaps a passive diff view leaves open.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
